{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2362 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "0ff48292436a94d7962a667f0c696fcca2df1277e93ceeda17654b4ab77c3c21" :edb/db-before-t 2361 :edb/db-after-t 2362 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535674 50 #inst "2026-10-09T04:52:59.632Z" 13194139535674 true] [17592186166753 1020 #edb/ref 17592186360567 13194139535674 true] [17592186166753 1020 #edb/ref 17592186360588 13194139535674 true] [17592186166754 1020 #edb/ref 17592186360574 13194139535674 true] [17592186166754 1020 #edb/ref 17592186360581 13194139535674 true] [17592186166789 1020 #edb/ref 17592186360595 13194139535674 true] [17592186360567 1017 #edb/ref 17592186360568 13194139535674 true] [17592186360567 1089 "q-115680" 13194139535674 true] [17592186360567 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535674 true] [17592186360567 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069297/assets/ac782575d30a0f87e5a930ae03ef91f9c606cc03baa5b4ff9037f871a63fccd9.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" 13194139535674 true] [17592186360567 1113 #uuid "692cfb5d-4022-59e1-9118-b329cfd0c2b9" 13194139535674 true] [17592186360567 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069297/assets/f2ad5a7c6ea5a224583246a32e0da6de66c834f4d87be1b5bc646d3c7a678163.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=-2$ and $x=4.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should equal zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+2)^{2}(4-x) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =(x+2)(x-4)^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =3x(x+2)(x-4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can dismiss the equation ${y}^{′}=3x(x+2)(x-4)^{2}.$ Although the right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-2$ and $x=4,$ it also evaluates to zero when $x=0,$ which is behavior not exhibited by our slope field.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=-2$ and $x=4$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x<-2$ | $-2<x<4$ | $x>4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2}(x+2)^{2}(4-x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2}(x+2)^{2}(4-x).$" 13194139535674 true] [17592186360568 1000 #edb/ref 17592186360569 13194139535674 true] [17592186360568 1000 #edb/ref 17592186360570 13194139535674 true] [17592186360568 1000 #edb/ref 17592186360571 13194139535674 true] [17592186360568 1000 #edb/ref 17592186360572 13194139535674 true] [17592186360568 1000 #edb/ref 17592186360573 13194139535674 true] [17592186360568 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535674 true] [17592186360568 1041 "selection" 13194139535674 true] [17592186360568 1104 #edb/ref 17592186360570 13194139535674 true] [17592186360568 1110 #uuid "57b45219-5e5b-5375-b470-70dca3deab45" 13194139535674 true] [17592186360569 1034 "{y}^{′}=(x+2)^{2}" 13194139535674 true] [17592186360569 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535674 true] [17592186360569 1111 #uuid "d20a6edc-0b56-5519-a58a-f0283047aacb" 13194139535674 true] [17592186360570 1034 "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2}(x+2)^{2}(4-x)" 13194139535674 true] [17592186360570 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535674 true] [17592186360570 1111 #uuid "6ebf00d3-3445-578b-8668-59f876e606bc" 13194139535674 true] [17592186360571 1034 "{y}^{′}=3x(x+2)(x-4)" 13194139535674 true] [17592186360571 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535674 true] [17592186360571 1111 #uuid "886a0ce8-3f20-59a1-9a36-8b939388b11e" 13194139535674 true] [17592186360572 1034 "{y}^{′}=(x+2)(x-4)^{2}" 13194139535674 true] [17592186360572 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535674 true] [17592186360572 1111 #uuid "c1d030f5-1f64-5b10-b9f5-9006f93918da" 13194139535674 true] [17592186360573 1034 "{y}^{′}=2(x-2)(x-4)" 13194139535674 true] [17592186360573 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535674 true] [17592186360573 1111 #uuid "cca2a13a-ad44-530b-942d-c12dd1df933c" 13194139535674 true] [17592186360574 1017 #edb/ref 17592186360575 13194139535674 true] [17592186360574 1089 "q-115754" 13194139535674 true] [17592186360574 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535674 true] [17592186360574 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069297/assets/34af8d1078100aea331c300ea529d2fdefbdd31e99ee8a7946325d58a8abf680.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=7-{x}^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{x+7}$\n- ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{3}{x+7}$" 13194139535674 true] [17592186360574 1113 #uuid "b507f238-cfcf-5089-bfe0-5c0eb3e8d978" 13194139535674 true] [17592186360574 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069297/assets/d02883b776dbcd55c5c96347ed1a7bb0e51ef8c26e62786049b6585b878d04fb.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The solution curves have the vertical asymptote $x=-7.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=7-{x}^{2}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side does not approach $\\pm \\infty $ as $x\\to -7.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{x+7}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{x+7}=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{(1+\\frac{7}{x})}\\to -\\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{3}{x+7}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n-\\frac{3}{x+7}\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139535674 true] [17592186360575 1000 #edb/ref 17592186360576 13194139535674 true] [17592186360575 1000 #edb/ref 17592186360577 13194139535674 true] [17592186360575 1000 #edb/ref 17592186360578 13194139535674 true] [17592186360575 1000 #edb/ref 17592186360579 13194139535674 true] [17592186360575 1000 #edb/ref 17592186360580 13194139535674 true] [17592186360575 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535674 true] [17592186360575 1041 "selection" 13194139535674 true] [17592186360575 1104 #edb/ref 17592186360579 13194139535674 true] [17592186360575 1110 #uuid "5adaf710-fbe4-5980-adc6-dbbee2f20e9c" 13194139535674 true] [17592186360576 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535674 true] [17592186360576 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535674 true] [17592186360576 1111 #uuid "7c388f6d-06c3-56ed-894c-413649f71971" 13194139535674 true] [17592186360577 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535674 true] [17592186360577 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535674 true] [17592186360577 1111 #uuid "8ab39b6f-f235-5b4c-8f1e-b1228a89c75f" 13194139535674 true] [17592186360578 1034 "I only" 13194139535674 true] [17592186360578 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535674 true] [17592186360578 1111 #uuid "65edacab-7ff2-5a88-a7a0-94eee027bde4" 13194139535674 true] [17592186360579 1034 "III only" 13194139535674 true] [17592186360579 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535674 true] [17592186360579 1111 #uuid "0d8ab2fa-5a90-5ba3-a296-416e05865e03" 13194139535674 true] [17592186360580 1034 "II only" 13194139535674 true] [17592186360580 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535674 true] [17592186360580 1111 #uuid "eefcb27e-5e07-5e48-8515-f487bdba4b2d" 13194139535674 true] [17592186360581 1017 #edb/ref 17592186360582 13194139535674 true] [17592186360581 1089 "q-115854" 13194139535674 true] [17592186360581 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535674 true] [17592186360581 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069297/assets/753554f8129ab0ed83f19e5cadefba27cb3b700818bd3a3f801c64aaf01ded70.png)\n\nIdentify the differential equation that generated the slope field above." 13194139535674 true] [17592186360581 1113 #uuid "89e18e48-808b-5b06-800c-06851f8a0fc9" 13194139535674 true] [17592186360581 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069297/assets/f26e5b61c573664f0397667aff82cfe5d918e0d22f005658862c28c53d5de748.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the solution curves have vertical asymptotes $x=2$ and $x=4.$\n\nOf the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-6x+8}=\\frac{1}{(x-2)(x-4)}\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}-6x+8}=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{(x-2)(x-4)}.\n$$\n\n- Second, the slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$ \n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-6x+8}.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-6x+8}\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-6x+8}$ become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nOn the other hand,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}-6x+8}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{(1-\\frac{6}{x}+\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}})}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}-6x+8}$ do *not* become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$" 13194139535674 true] [17592186360582 1000 #edb/ref 17592186360583 13194139535674 true] [17592186360582 1000 #edb/ref 17592186360584 13194139535674 true] [17592186360582 1000 #edb/ref 17592186360585 13194139535674 true] [17592186360582 1000 #edb/ref 17592186360586 13194139535674 true] [17592186360582 1000 #edb/ref 17592186360587 13194139535674 true] [17592186360582 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535674 true] [17592186360582 1041 "selection" 13194139535674 true] [17592186360582 1104 #edb/ref 17592186360587 13194139535674 true] [17592186360582 1110 #uuid "c49507a2-8e68-5e3c-8b2d-bea589b06489" 13194139535674 true] [17592186360583 1034 "{y}^{′}=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}-6x+8}" 13194139535674 true] [17592186360583 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535674 true] [17592186360583 1111 #uuid "685a799e-0458-5fc0-b9f7-507e356bfdda" 13194139535674 true] [17592186360584 1034 "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-4}" 13194139535674 true] [17592186360584 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535674 true] [17592186360584 1111 #uuid "ffb4c567-db8d-5e15-80f9-6c4bf25aace3" 13194139535674 true] [17592186360585 1034 "{y}^{′}=\\frac{x-4}{x-2}" 13194139535674 true] [17592186360585 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535674 true] [17592186360585 1111 #uuid "165edf21-9ad5-5fa5-9dc5-f2bcdf27e7e0" 13194139535674 true] [17592186360586 1034 "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x-4}" 13194139535674 true] [17592186360586 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535674 true] [17592186360586 1111 #uuid "930f182c-6108-54dc-a387-0fe85d5341d6" 13194139535674 true] [17592186360587 1034 "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-6x+8}" 13194139535674 true] [17592186360587 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535674 true] [17592186360587 1111 #uuid "a770575a-f9e0-5ee8-884b-ff606def3fbd" 13194139535674 true] [17592186360588 1017 #edb/ref 17592186360589 13194139535674 true] [17592186360588 1089 "q-120038" 13194139535674 true] [17592186360588 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535674 true] [17592186360588 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069297/assets/f855062048a7d38b2fa6c843701a182661b2235b2042ad83fbedd600b1dd770c.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{10}(x+3)(x-2)^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{5}(x+3)(2-x)$\n- ${y}^{′}=(x+2)(x-3)$" 13194139535674 true] [17592186360588 1113 #uuid "cdeb6520-9bfc-52b8-b6bf-9364334ae71a" 13194139535674 true] [17592186360588 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069297/assets/8fdec9bda9e2bcc514e56a14a6608b2f0cb74fd0943a51c944b29cc047e02cd9.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=-3$ and $x=2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=-3$ and $x=2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x<-3$ | $-3<x<2$ | $x>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{10}(x+3)(x-2)^{2}$ gives the required behavior.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{5}(x+3)(2-x)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-3$ and $x=2,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| Region | $x<-3$ | $-3<x<2$ | $x>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=(x+2)(x-3)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-2$ and $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer i